#uuid "7bf20b01-76bc-543c-a0e8-b3bf2f4563c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326684 :question/id #uuid "d55b44c8-8d42-5261-b22a-10b38f009304"} :step/id #uuid "9b37de30-2d0f-54d1-9978-97a780796cdb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494019}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493385}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175780 :question/id #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" :question/math-academy-id "e-5527" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n![](images/cc/cc6a84d8a040240089d946999190e4d118fee2be70df2958a6a59d6534bcf429.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/1c/1c7fa984e0d00619ea2e8647f560efc3f928b643875f0cbf973ca8bb2a931e3e.svg)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](images/ec/ec3f75c25f412d466b7acfc4810ecf6987038a23ee5da0dfdd98cc181e37e0c7.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8b2061-c287-5c3c-a92f-b50b775811f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324442} {:answer/id #uuid "42816b74-d9d4-56ee-a023-c3ef727ffd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324507} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8e4e14-6644-52e2-80c1-057e345662a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325019} {:answer/id #uuid "e454e0c2-b305-5d79-84ae-55356c79cdbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325981} {:answer/id #uuid "fb961200-3140-5d15-9631-200124ac1dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325019} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d4316c-037a-5deb-94ff-27d238034814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326467}] :question/id #uuid "74f2c3c7-9e59-5362-99fc-fbdc0df5c116" :question/math-academy-id "q-38477" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-3\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+(3-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-3\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{1-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(1+\\text{i})}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4bff84-a629-50e4-9829-658a3e69e55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324349} {:answer/id #uuid "488f687d-fb5e-529b-a155-9e4450d9f98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324562} {:answer/id #uuid "65fca3bd-5fb8-5962-943c-9041f3194fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324818} {:answer/id #uuid "776862d6-c908-51d2-b710-f7385d35a46f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324964} {:answer/id #uuid "81484b01-f530-5ee6-8cad-17fefb473669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324818} :answer-field/id #uuid "548ec993-677b-573b-b294-2c206e8f6a07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326365}] :question/id #uuid "795927e9-7dd6-5d7f-82cf-cc21ad14ed8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39185" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10+2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(10+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a00c60-8fe9-5976-9772-04b53a4f0cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269556} {:answer/id #uuid "28840bf8-728e-529f-b8ae-9e144824501a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269633} {:answer/id #uuid "4501ee0a-1e3d-5b90-8c4a-0918a3d52df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269901} {:answer/id #uuid "6b565f0d-2ee2-522d-abd4-444d8a903c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270223} {:answer/id #uuid "99f68ace-9f93-55a7-be71-674b44605a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b48799-b04a-51df-82be-a32acaaa2f29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271870}] :question/id #uuid "015b39ab-328e-51c1-8903-e3d3a737ea7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140385" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218e7f86-fd76-5b62-824c-83878e5bd18a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269554} {:answer/id #uuid "4553eb41-3d9c-52c0-ba89-8dff0e063c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269903} {:answer/id #uuid "a54f682b-cdc2-5a38-8d92-2296471a4276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270748} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e33566-b4c7-5384-8d52-c2e8c364ec65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270783} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f233d2-431d-5443-9348-f83d4b7ad575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270783} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04d4fe9-24df-5216-a1a9-6654c178e23a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271903}] :question/id #uuid "6e2d30bd-f2f6-597b-88fa-fb1c99f06b89" :question/math-academy-id "q-140386" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3+\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(3+\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3+\\text{i}}{5}=-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13dd2318-4acd-5f49-96e4-964560faa20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\text{i}-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324151} {:answer/id #uuid "30c10b40-14b2-50b7-85ac-154daea05d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324360} {:answer/id #uuid "5fddcd10-b697-5b22-89d3-1b693101de9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324772} {:answer/id #uuid "74edc1c1-a147-5f13-a167-4e9be86d5e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324943} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c4138f-36ac-5a64-b035-d78d6b81eb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324943} :answer-field/id #uuid "a410cc5b-840f-5ca0-a324-ef088fd08daf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326514}] :question/id #uuid "d39fbf55-bc06-5e3f-bb6c-75c8e166f893" :question/math-academy-id "q-38287" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{-1}=\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e6770c-7bf9-5f46-9062-0e30bf90a42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348592} {:answer/id #uuid "6a72311c-58cb-5b33-9b66-4446eb52c4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349429} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bf0977-5fa5-50f0-be87-c64eb8d03d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350045} {:answer/id #uuid "c7687cf8-d801-5c98-be83-eb0c3229d84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350202} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5e115e-8718-54b9-9627-bd37a0f17f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350252} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e4857a-4c14-5c14-8928-81cdfeac6383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350801}] :question/id #uuid "7207a8c6-6c0f-53ed-83a0-48cf577c32a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81027" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a2284a-eb43-562f-941d-87d7220072bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348525} {:answer/id #uuid "33f237e1-cab4-5534-9c5e-564a219d644a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348912} {:answer/id #uuid "3677b971-e762-54fc-8686-774c54d9c5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348936} {:answer/id #uuid "6698deed-e683-54d3-abf8-3138a1e168e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349387} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8da2a3-34e6-59f2-9106-88c389c4ae28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348912} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ed531d-4a4e-51da-a1d0-c7db20e48f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350743}] :question/id #uuid "bcaaf961-40fb-5a48-94d0-be1d8933da54" :question/math-academy-id "q-81030" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242999fb-6645-5ccb-8016-d94893b454bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348760} {:answer/id #uuid "492ab36c-e111-5bf5-aecb-2c775badfc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349110} {:answer/id #uuid "497ebdce-d955-50f6-9c24-16eb97943ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349113} {:answer/id #uuid "8a583dc2-aca0-5635-8def-a18f404d931b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349701} {:answer/id #uuid "fafa63b9-44ba-538d-855c-38a2f923e2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349113} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b83764-dadd-53c8-9a1e-0eaa7017e5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351116}] :question/id #uuid "d7f8f4ed-a707-5ef8-a488-4f074f1c04ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-81031" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c441ed-0435-5e2d-83a0-618a3112fcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348499} {:answer/id #uuid "45842989-10cc-5c73-ad09-63becc6e2b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349079} {:answer/id #uuid "4662c9e9-26e1-5ad9-933f-23f51efda70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349088} {:answer/id #uuid "5d944e2a-38b8-5891-a36e-9e4f87137e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349308} {:answer/id #uuid "806a25dd-fc2a-5451-8d21-5511e240733a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349624} :answer-field/id #uuid "390a9ffd-1e61-5aac-8fcf-72786b0b334b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350805}] :question/id #uuid "931d7f2e-4fc8-5dd3-95f9-e9f514cf8458" :question/math-academy-id "q-81184" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0489168e-93f4-5458-8a02-7bdcd1fa6253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348487} {:answer/id #uuid "4f637c61-750b-5562-b8aa-12c7f8e8f1af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349164} {:answer/id #uuid "733e337e-a48f-5c12-902f-9d1678192174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349506} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6761e2-57cb-5fcc-9965-269be85a60a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350374} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b3ce77-31f6-5c5b-9f4d-8e85c892abd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350374} :answer-field/id #uuid "674d76e0-0dad-52e1-9b92-041ef1633c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350876}] :question/id #uuid "fbfe9a5f-3d3d-54aa-9e82-2b9e587c84ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-81185" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3\\text{i}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{i}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bff17e-251a-51b2-8967-41537af47d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349071} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5be9b6-047d-523a-9c7b-b70df97eba20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349289} {:answer/id #uuid "64aa7359-5c20-533a-a7b7-a61f8bc791cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349370} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9a246e-317d-59ea-ab76-f63812c61026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349596} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a0b76-0a49-5fdd-88de-6ccdd6c60a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349071} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e1e6a3-e7b4-5b9d-a1df-519d12657f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350966}] :question/id #uuid "e54214f9-057a-5459-9533-979d032ad9ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-81187" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1+3\\text{i}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63270d13-a486-5e65-86a2-26efe3de1a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394121} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d0f326-bfd3-514a-9f2e-d732dbe798a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394122} {:answer/id #uuid "e72e51bc-0717-5dc9-bb50-7476df4a1034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394123} {:answer/id #uuid "998ad9ff-ea3d-5e57-9576-469b13fb13ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394124} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1d2f82-0269-5ecf-bdf0-b7a14bd760c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394121} :answer-field/id #uuid "343b2826-9549-593c-b4af-0154e0f75219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394120}] :question/id #uuid "97d122ff-efb2-5263-88bb-fc5aa3dbdfd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81028" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+2\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175781 :question/id #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5528" :question/problem "Determine whether ![](images/85/8594aa29c1348769c738a5ed407df28d27324d8e6d8679971b3a5825de6aac68.svg) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](images/81/8111e3afdd5628fe312f17d6632b2d8c5204ddc9e7141e2e167ea560aad55e83.svg)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e413879-bad2-5459-b815-b58af8fe06ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324101} {:answer/id #uuid "31305125-c1a7-5562-b008-9cb4ca098e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324366} {:answer/id #uuid "453a251f-5d76-59f6-bac5-83a29514a3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324531} {:answer/id #uuid "660dd11c-d44c-5521-9fcc-1a3b1f9a3ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324819} {:answer/id #uuid "684105b6-be85-5008-ae7f-733f6f8b7fd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324101} :answer-field/id #uuid "f73ff81e-6976-583b-adde-1fc594bd1ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326661}] :question/id #uuid "c6beb736-d02a-5b38-8238-42457ab554c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38475" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\text{i} \\\\ 6-6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\text{i} \\\\ -6{\\text{i}}^{2}-6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=3\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff731292-d0a7-5a02-8229-24bf6fdc3022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175591} {:answer/id #uuid "05a52154-a704-5e15-a11d-70a80d71913b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434766} {:answer/id #uuid "395b71eb-934f-50c8-901b-d659116b2b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434767} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8367f4-f25a-5797-be99-f2deef2ca2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434768} {:answer/id #uuid "58a69195-600a-5e46-b79b-827cb7f39b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434769} :answer-field/id #uuid "714d9cd6-ccc3-57b3-a248-3dddcfab66af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175588}] :question/id #uuid "7491fb8c-9ad3-530d-9662-34320e8d6d88" :question/math-academy-id "q-81146" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{\\text{i}}^{2}+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}(2-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 2(2-2\\text{i})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a6f76a5-081e-5aa9-8608-faf741cd472e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269482} {:answer/id #uuid "3758cf04-ed36-5e10-af4c-432af9b120da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269770} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee910d8-646c-58ad-86de-fa39e799bdc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3+4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270110} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c2831c-b9a8-5be6-a364-53ba38ec3b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270884} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4ea31f-51e0-5a5e-9150-adb8600ee36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269482} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dde7a94-7233-5432-b670-50a9201f979f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271530}] :question/id #uuid "ed8541a0-a9d9-5421-b327-f593facee158" :question/math-academy-id "q-140383" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}(4+3\\text{i}) \\\\ (-1)(4+3\\text{i})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4+3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22610554-24a3-59dc-aecd-489c93f58250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269565} {:answer/id #uuid "447f5eff-619c-5208-8270-f8cf786477fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269891} {:answer/id #uuid "64e15afb-96b7-51d4-acc4-264a85f8d6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270166} {:answer/id #uuid "882d89c9-e342-55ae-b014-e3ebca9dfbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270448} {:answer/id #uuid "8db7f09f-cb3f-5a33-b717-3b583d00ee34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269565} :answer-field/id #uuid "34dab9b1-2f48-5c2c-849e-6f87a8d5c931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271609}] :question/id #uuid "f770f6e7-5001-51dd-b514-c38647e15145" :question/math-academy-id "q-140384" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i}(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(2-3\\text{i})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2369e57e-0838-59a4-a907-6471525b4592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324262} {:answer/id #uuid "79fe92d7-0e20-5d1a-9aa9-5b51a61046c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324989} {:answer/id #uuid "9554e27e-f29e-5c1a-8ec1-78b11640dbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325243} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8beff7-5c96-56ec-a386-69f16fe58f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325618} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f57107-05d0-5b45-b6d1-f2b3df4bdea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324262} :answer-field/id #uuid "eece9ac2-4fba-574f-91d1-d90d80b39b60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326650}] :question/id #uuid "d70dcd02-1fff-5601-a74f-2a4d94cea28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-38281" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\text{i} \\\\ -4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e967569-f453-574e-8919-a67fad95d2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324758} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6f6a10-0bd5-5a15-8100-84cdb1b48c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325009} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1224cc-38c3-56bc-8a03-7ccf158de176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325145} {:answer/id #uuid "ca079c89-eb27-58a1-8df2-a13f57b8062e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325722} {:answer/id #uuid "e251a6af-588a-5940-924b-907ba00680c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325009} :answer-field/id #uuid "d985c21d-6faa-5e92-8ab4-fa9bd7e30639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326615}] :question/id #uuid "0ea0f7da-291a-5ef5-afec-bc983b4386ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-39181" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-49-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}49{\\text{i}}^{2}-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=7\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481922c7-396d-5465-aaf3-948208f860f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349099} {:answer/id #uuid "80945101-f875-5085-9497-ff8518edc6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349629} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dfe530-8514-58a1-800c-d76f0047a94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349924} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2952cc-2272-549b-abb4-dfb108df6572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350111} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b00708-9b2c-5d24-9242-4331ad0ca03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349099} :answer-field/id #uuid "9266dd22-d51e-5d23-82bc-65bad5a4190b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350955}] :question/id #uuid "edb2c398-ec60-50c0-bce1-beafe9c44e8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81024" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{\\text{i}}^{2}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i}(\\text{i}+1) \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(1+\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39df06ba-7cff-5357-98f5-67fe920c81ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348968} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8a2d09-2bfa-5830-ad28-1ec59eb98735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349147} {:answer/id #uuid "5585850d-2c82-55d7-bcb1-8456eac120d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349230} {:answer/id #uuid "5df0ab3d-754d-5fb2-974d-b46807d1f736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349310} {:answer/id #uuid "bd28768c-71e5-51a4-91c6-da6247b6b126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348968} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc9abce-442e-5b58-9fa9-bb4764508a81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350860}] :question/id #uuid "46aa3476-303b-5222-af57-59b75f51d09f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81025" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006f21ef-bd9e-5319-853e-7e3fc38e4a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411578} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc31227-522c-5e3c-865f-858998fd51d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411579} {:answer/id #uuid "b38f6e8b-7681-58f7-b559-5a8bae4dff01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411580} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dc0bd2-9334-5cf0-9566-65f6c6862a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411581} {:answer/id #uuid "fa45e365-566e-56f7-88cc-77bf8156b5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411582} :answer-field/id #uuid "93c408a0-c9c5-54b6-81eb-ee9b40cb4448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411583}] :question/id #uuid "db7154e6-6fda-5907-98f7-b3398371be54" :question/math-academy-id "q-38493" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8+4\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27933876-5b2f-59dc-a5a5-942ee704758c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493454} {:answer/id #uuid "4695373b-5cc8-56e0-bcf9-e8857baa20e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493510} {:answer/id #uuid "7e689100-10cd-5e89-9858-aae9672b630a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493587} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3dfe99-6eeb-5edd-b06f-b616e7766a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493635} {:answer/id #uuid "af381ec0-91d6-5997-9ce5-2412f090232d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493587} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cfc4d0-a713-5f9b-8684-1ec443f7a134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493871}] :question/id #uuid "6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81148" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{\\text{i}}^{2}-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\text{i}(4-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(4-2\\text{i})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175782 :question/id #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5529" :question/problem "Let ![](images/53/5337ed67f212c0029ae2317c658672aa3f3f68a7614c8ce4dfafb6e92d2f2229.svg) and ![](images/4f/4fad8749e9d06a4cafdd089e6ed982131a8957fff201d865fb9da323c5c27bf5.svg) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](images/a0/a05c08cb757f62c9b874fce3b3ae9db1d4278f1f96194a3e92df0a4d457b591c.svg)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/33/334bab555d470ebcc0d36eb4e2140530d4f92b2fa13e4adbfea848250d92de7b.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](images/82/82d57d12bd8027fc3c7e9b368b752616ed33ffcce577b956c91cb8a721dd5905.svg) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](images/8c/8cd66a9324ee5cf95277457fc1eef8ddb30d36543b71d4446eb93b5a2d8313a7.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad718acf-bc34-5af5-ab86-3a5dc856b0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366067} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c5ebb-e7a9-57dc-ac9a-df4eb523b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366068} {:answer/id #uuid "7a336dcb-e67a-58d8-bb13-be3f3425f6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366069} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc1d8b9-1b3b-58d6-9550-65d587e7b93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366070} {:answer/id #uuid "53304843-707b-558e-bb98-11a42cfed664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366068} :answer-field/id #uuid "c472b1f5-b749-54e2-afc1-cb077c5b2380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366066}] :question/id #uuid "597496aa-43f1-55f6-97dc-9702fffad14f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38478" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector of ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c63e26d-074e-5c36-8b58-33b7f0f8843d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324084} {:answer/id #uuid "781d1a4b-1bc8-581a-92ed-92f43c30d4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324968} {:answer/id #uuid "7b971c04-f79d-51cf-bd5b-9f7c7631cab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325011} {:answer/id #uuid "acc11610-a434-5194-92fd-1427c352ec10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325438} {:answer/id #uuid "f04a03aa-bf67-5848-a19c-45ba01434156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326093} :answer-field/id #uuid "218338cd-5fd4-5bce-b4d9-5812bff2cbd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326277}] :question/id #uuid "b212f7d8-65d4-5b14-984d-a9ab26791d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-38480" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bba4cca-4b6d-54d9-b716-02eea9952cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269496} {:answer/id #uuid "34172e22-148d-5a66-b24d-4fecaf4cd955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269745} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae27467-b518-54f4-b08e-9ae85afdba59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269803} {:answer/id #uuid "8c76ddae-5668-51bd-93cb-012868ad8b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270489} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb16bd1-0fae-55ce-91eb-a51dd1f01622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269496} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc74aef3-dbd1-57dd-878a-1be1265f9c44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271945}] :question/id #uuid "c5031f0e-aebf-58c8-ad51-53ebefa342d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140387" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5-5\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 10 & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-5-5\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}+(5-5\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+5\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1-5\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}{(-2)(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011ee86d-97e1-53d3-a3b6-63ede2abaf4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} {:answer/id #uuid "06c071d9-5963-5866-8063-d0604b2c6652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269329} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b6a67-1956-5bd6-bf81-18c5c5717aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270464} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8004b4-6cab-5417-9d66-a6e51002060e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270640} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a5fd11-7f6b-58c9-94e4-6d1dc39b30af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} :answer-field/id #uuid "21591163-e34d-5795-bc94-436e957ba0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271570}] :question/id #uuid "8845c35f-ffc0-56f1-b7af-e5bdef685e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140388" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2373d10f-9177-5caa-aa21-81933675524a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324263} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0c4ffc-7cb8-5890-8b26-5fc5ba15fdf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324333} {:answer/id #uuid "87d22551-71cd-562b-a2fb-e2c71dff8be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325128} {:answer/id #uuid "991ed941-102e-554e-9c2b-837d919cb7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325274} {:answer/id #uuid "f8014819-4600-55cf-996f-8ea4d1e77dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325274} :answer-field/id #uuid "afc9f365-fbcc-5a5c-bd09-b46393c41e88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326538}] :question/id #uuid "d55b44c8-8d42-5261-b22a-10b38f009304" :question/math-academy-id "q-38494" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071cf655-9ba4-53ce-b6ef-2eeacc1f754c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324037} {:answer/id #uuid "319cb4e4-41ae-5830-8a9d-016acde7074d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324373} {:answer/id #uuid "654cb5c8-10e7-5237-87e7-ea9f1eee9272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324809} {:answer/id #uuid "756c139a-8995-536e-a52f-dc3cac4c8819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324946} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0baf96-4aee-5473-bca6-50ce323afec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325730} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b7fefe-d170-5fa7-a7f6-ad95f63e8c13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326359}] :question/id #uuid "f4febda2-345b-5458-b675-3b62332f742d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39187" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6+3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -15 & -6+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(6+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+(-6+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{\\text{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3952c809-b36e-5c0a-8130-4cc5c4fb5968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324433} {:answer/id #uuid "43c02d07-8760-5ae6-9e7a-56ace78318f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value 